  "query_preview": "..A seasonably cool start to the morning across the \nregion, with some patchy fog noted in places such as the Uinta \nBasin, Cache Valley, Bear River Valley, and other some other \nsheltered mountain valleys. This will continue to lift and scatter\nout as the day progresses. Mid to upper level cloud cover is also\ngradually increasing as a shortwave trough continues to move in. \nMoisture with this feature is pretty limited overall, but high \nresolution models do at least carry a low end chance at some \nisolated showers from around mid afternoon to evening for portions\nof northern Utah to southwest Wyoming. The best chances are noted\nacross the higher terrain, especially the Uintas. Would also not \nentirely rule out some patchy fog development late night into the \nmorning again in similar areas that saw fog development this \nmorning.\n\nImpacts wise, the only thing of note with this system is some \ndevelopment of modest wind gusts around 25-45 mph across portions \nof the Pine Valley Mountains and gaps into favorably SW to NE \noriented canyons (such as Black Ridge) as the trough sinks deeper \ninto the Four Corners region. These look to increase by Friday \nmorning, become maximized late Friday evening, and finally \ndecrease through the overnight hours.\n\nOverall, ridging gradually becomes the dominant forecast \ninfluence moving towards the weekend and on into the upcoming \nweek. This will allow for a period of drier and more stable \nweather, with temperatures gradually warming back to above \nseasonal normal. A few grazing systems look to bring periods of \nincreased cloud cover, but precipitation chances will remain very \nlow.\n\nModels show fairly good consensus in precipitation chances not \nreally increasing until around Thursday next week at the earliest.\nEven then, there is a good deal of spread in those details of how\nany such potential system moves in, how much moisture accompanies\nit, and how long any unsettled period would last. Still, at least\nthere remains a favorable signal for something in that extended \nrange.",
